In article <035331Z03121994@anon.penet.fi> an35605@anon.penet.fi "Leda" writes:

>We were recognized last May by the Student Governing Board of Earl Hall, 
>and began meeting in Sept. In November a petition was submitted asking the 
>executive board of SGB to reconsider our status because a) we'd been 
>misrepresented at the meeting at which we were originally recognized and 
>b) we did not fit into the mission of Earl Hall.  We met with the Exec. board 
>and then were informed that they'd disbanded us based on those two charges, 
>plus one which we had not had the opportunity to answer: that we promote 
>"unjustifiable violence."

That the SGB should have judged you on a charge of which you had not been 
previously informed is a fundamental denial of one of your basic human rights. 
Likewise, for them to have decided to disband the group without first of all 
allowing the group even to speak in its defence or, better, to confront (or be 
confronted by) its accusers is also a denial of fundamental human rights.

It is no defence to that charge of human rights abuse that a subsequent appeal 
might have been permitted to be heard -- the original decision to disband was 
made in violation of the human rights of the group and of the members of that 
group. Nor is it any defence to try to argue that, as Columbia or "Earl Hall" 
is a private body, they may wilfully violate the human rights of whom they 
choose, for *nobody* may expect to violate the fundamental human rights of 
someone else without expecting to meet with retribution for those actions.

>Subsequently it was discovered that they'd been operating on an obsolete 
>version of the bylaws, and that the decision had to be ratified by a 
>simple majority of the whole SGB to be official. that ratification took 
>place last night.

And, thus, the whole SGB has become a party to the violation of your human 
rights.

Under these circumstances, I would suggest that the SGB itself be disbanded, 
for, by denying the rights to fair trial and due process, it has surely voided 
any and all rights to govern.

>What we're doing (so far):
>We have already started the appeals process  - to the Earl Hall advisory 
>board. According to the director of Earl Hall no one really knows whether the 
>advisory board has the power to overrule the students, so we'll see.

But they *can* cut off funding, presumably?  If so, they should cut off the 
funding of those so-called "Christian" groups who would so cheerfully take the 
parts of the Pharisees and Saducees in denying simple, basic human rights to 
others, whose viewpoint happens to differ from their own.

>TammyJo...says: I feel that these "Christian" groups, by refusing to talk 
>to us at any time during the semester, by starting and signing a secret 
>petition against us, by rudely insulting our members when we called to 
>inform all members of Earl Hall when the vote was, and by not listening to 
>the arguments but basing their votes on rumor have 
>all behaved in a very unChristlike manner. 

Indeed, but what else is new?  For a long while now, the more conservative 
parts of the Christian church have offered a safe haven for those who would 
cheerfully impose their will upon their fellow men. One might call such people 
"fascists", but they don't really have the style of their European 
counterparts and, in consequence, they're just boorish thugs.

I'd suppose that people like that might well have been in Boston at the time of 
that tea-party there, but whether they'd be on the decks of the ships hurling 
the tea into the harbor, or whether they'd be swimming about in the water and 
trying to put the tea back onto the ships, I'd really not like to say. Heck, 
they might even have been rowing about the harbor in dinghies and skiffs, 
salvaging what tea they could so that they might then sell it back to the 
English at a profit!

They are a pestilential and suppurating excrescence on the face of America.

< Paul >

