From henry@charger.newhaven.edu Tue Dec  6 19:08:42 EST 1994

I am responding to a complaint by a user of my system at the request of my
boss.  What guidelines have you instituted regulating what users may store
or transmit on your systems?

1.) Do you censor .plan contents?  I have a user with an ASCII picture of
    someone giving the world the finger.

2.) What about .sig-s?  A particular one encourages drug use at odds with
    the university's printed drug policy.  Actually I believe she's being 
    factious or humorous, but a lawyer may not concur.   

3.) Another user (this is what got mailed to my boss, passed to me and is
    generating this inquiry) posted a mail message stating how to fake an
    email address and the wording specifically said that this was how
    "you can harass someone and not get caught".  Okay, the instructions
    were wrong <g> and I'd already safeguarded against direct telnets to
    port 25 but my boss had a baby when he heard about it.

Here's my take:

1-A.)  Commercial advertising should be discouraged in .plans or .sigs.  
       Racial or any other type of abuse should be prohibited.
1-B.)  If a siggie is offensive enough someone will reply to the postmaster
       when they get mail, so I submit that that's the appropriate time to 
       be concerned.  The same goes for plans.  If no one fingers you it's 
       not an issue.  If they read it and get offended they can, again, send 
       mail to the postmaster.

Conclusion:
     Unlike a virus, making backups or anticipating security holes, there's 
     no need to create an issue until it becomes a concern.  


2-A.)  I have over 350 users (on a MSDOS box running Linux no less! :-).
       I have better things to do than worry about the kinds of plans and
       sigs that undergrads are historically predisposed to create.
2-B.)  Hey, I'm the SysAdmin, not the Morals Czar.  If you want to post
       something that you'll regret if you ever run for office that's
       your business, not mine.

Conclusion:
       It's neither practical nor (arguably) any of my business to poke
       around in private storage areas or attempt to make the contents 
       conform to my personal standards or my interpertation of law or 
       policy... until circumstances force me to.
 
And yet...

1.)  I don't want to get sued and I don't want the university to get sued.
2.)  I work hard to craft and maintain this system and I feel a personal
     stake in not having any part of it look like a bathroom wall.
3.)  I'm new to System Administration.  I don't want to be naive in my
     resolve to be as unlike the "Nazi System Administrator" model as I
     can.  I don't want to cause myself more trouble later.      

So...
   My boss and I agree that we will create the minimum possible number of
rules and guidelines.  We will talk to users directly when situations 
arise- and only create regs when all polite efforts have failed.

But before we talk to current users we want our policy in place so we
can judge what we want to say to them! 

What rules, procedures and guidelines have you instituted to deal with the
above situations and what experiences led you to do so? 

Thank you, in advance, for your time and cooperation.

