	Take pride rather in that you may not agree with what they say,
but you'll defend their right to say it.

>3.)  I'm new to System Administration.  I don't want to be naive in my
>     resolve to be as unlike the "Nazi System Administrator" model as I
>     can.  I don't want to cause myself more trouble later.      

	Stick to the mantra "This is not my job". I won't say it won't
give you some trouble when a controversy erupts, but I think it
minimizes trouble overall.

>What rules, procedures and guidelines have you instituted to deal with the
>above situations and what experiences led you to do so? 

	I'm not rendering an official opinion here, but my experience is
that at MIT, files are consider the property of the user, and the user
is responsible for them, not the systems people. The system people will
only change a user's file under VERY extreme circumstances. I recommend
other places adopt similar policies.

