CITIHOPE INTERNATIONAL: BACKGROUND ON BOSNIA CHILDREN RESCUE MISSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Bosnia Children Rescue in Partnership with
The Solomon Children's Foundation -- Help Needed from Concerned 
Individuals, Corporations, Organizations and Churches

December 5, 1994: Zagreb, Croatia.

This mission concerns the long term care and placement of children 
orphaned, or otherwise abandoned, as a result of the Armed conflict in 
the Republic of BiH.

BACKGROUND
-------------------------------
Citihope International's affiliate, Solomon Children's Foundation (SCF), 
has a long and successful involvement of extending assistance to 
endangered children in Bosnia.  In August 1992, SCF was the first Non-
Governmental Agency (NGO) to independently deliver medical supplies to 
the Children's ward of the main Hospital in Sarajevo, by an overland 
route.  This involved crossing the main front lines of the opposing 
forces and the dangerous no-man's land, between the Serb forces and the 
Bosnians defending Sarajevo.

The delivery of supplies was warmly received, as the anesthetics that 
were delivered enabled Doctors to operate on Children who had received 
wounds, while the children were anesthetized.  Prior to our arrival, 
some children had to have been operated on without anesthetic.  While in 
Sarajevo, the SCF group came under direct fire on several occasions, and 
their accommodation was destroyed by rocket fire.

The SCF representatives were able to meet with the Mayor of Sarajevo and 
the Minister of Social Welfare.  At that times discussions were held 
concerning SCF offering care for Bosnian children, the offer was 
gratefully received and formed the basis for later contacts.

The account of this successful first trip was widely reported and this 
encouraged several other Relief Agencies to begin the dangerous 
independent overland delivery of Aid.

THE MISSION
-------------------------------
SCF has kept in contact with the BiH Ministry of Social Welfare and in 
recent months our contact has resulted in an invitation to receive a 
group of Bosnian children for Fostercare and eventual long-term 
placement.  The present state of affairs in the War Zones has 
dramatically intensified the need.

The children are presently in Bosnia, and we would have to collect them 
from the Bihac and Tuzla area.  These areas are precariously under the 
control of the BiH army.  However, the Tuzla region is surrounded by 
Serbian and Croat positions and Bihac is under intense attack by Serbian 
forces.

The need now is to act quickly before the situation deteriorates 
further.  Our present window of opportunity could be open only for days 
rather than weeks.  AS THIS PROPOSAL IS BEING TYPED WE CAN HEAR THE 
SHELLING AND SMALL ARMS FIRE.

We are able to place a rescue team in Bosnia within 72 hours and as such 
are only waiting for confirmation of available funding for the project.  
We understand the risks but our relief workers are willing to go in and 
accomplish this Mission.

The Croatian Movement for Christian Democracy, which is an 
interdenominational group seeking to represent Christian standards and 
ethical values in politics and public life in Croatia, has publicly 
endorsed our proposals and has written to the Bosnian Government 
encouraging them to work with us.  The CMCD is run on a day-by-day basis 
by a leading Croatian Attorney, and he has written us a legal brief 
explaining that according to the existing legal system in Bosnia, and 
Croatia, our proposals are in the best interests of the children 
concerned and are also completely in accord with the relevant laws.

Solomon Children's Foundation is registered as an approved Child Welfare 
Agency in Croatia.

THE CRITICAL NEED: RESCUE THE MOST AT-RISK CHILDREN, IMMEDIATELY
----------------------------------------------------------------
It is vitally important that we respond to the Bosnians' request quickly 
as every lost day brings with it greater danger and risk.  As of this 
time, we have the ability to collect the most at-risk children and bring 
them to safety.  We just need to assure the Bosnian authorities that 
adequate funds are in trust to rescue, transport and provide care for 
the children..

The immediate funding required would be for leased vehicles and 
transportation costs, for staff members on the mission, including 
placing a representative in Sarajevo to ensure continued Bosnian 
Government co-operation.  Medical and Fostercare provision for the 
children would be supplemented by individual gifts and special grants 
until permanency proposals could be finalized for the children involved.

We have received invitations to apply for funding in part from the UN 
High Commissioner for Refugees and Mercy International.  We have also 
secured a small grant from another US-based charitable organization.  
However, to ensure the viability of the Mission for the minimum of eight 
months that it will need to run, we need to secure at least another 
$50,000.00 immediately.

The funds are needed to be able to begin the evacuation of the children 
and secure their Fostercare in Croatia.  We would therefore need to 
transport the children through territory held by the Serbian forces or 
the HVO, and then into Croatia proper.

The Serbs and Croats normally allow the transit of refugees who are en-
route to another country, or who are being supported by foreign 
sponsors.  We recognize there is always risk to life and limb when 
implementing a humanitarian rescue in a war zone: our staff are fully 
committed to accomplishing the task.

The Bosnians have sent SCF a fax in the last few days requesting that we 
advise them of whether we are able to receive children quickly.  This is 
in response to the escalation of hostilities in Bosnia.

At this time we can contract with the Government of the BiH to supply 
Fostercare and permanent placement on the basis of our present 
negotiations.

The next step is to send our representative to Sarajevo to secure the 
provisions of the contract between ourselves and the Bosnians.  
Immediately and concurrent to that we must begin the process of 
assessment of the children in the Bosnian camps, and then to arrange 
their immediate evacuation.

To conduct the assessment and evacuation, we must send a vehicle with 
two members of suitably trained and experienced staff, to the Tuzla 
area.  UnProFo, will allow the vehicle and team to be escorted in by 
British members of UnProFo.  If UN forces are withdrawn from Bosnia, we 
will have to join a BiH convoy.

