In article <3c2ejg$cht@babyblue.cs.yale.edu>, henry@charger.newhaven.edu
(Henry W. Farkas) wrote:

> I am responding to a complaint by a user of my system at the request of my
> boss.  What guidelines have you instituted regulating what users may store
> or transmit on your systems?
> 
> 1.) Do you censor .plan contents?  I have a user with an ASCII picture of
>     someone giving the world the finger.
> 
(and various other questions)

We take the position that speech via computer is the same as any other
kind of speech on campus.  Therefore, we refer to the general campus
policy.  It's too hard to specify what passes or fails the University
criteria.  (Most everything passes anyway.)

Speech can cross the line into harassment and other kinds of disapproved
behavior, but the computer medium is not important.  Offensive material
can and does appear on any number of physical bulletin boards, sidewalks,
etc.

We do have a policy that defines "appropriate use" of our facilities. 
Without appropriate approval, we disallow activities for profit or on
behalf of non-university organizations, etc.  If a user hogs disk space
with pornography, that is pretty much the same as hogging it with anything
else.

When it comes to behavior directed against our facilities and users, like
cracking, impersonation, resource hogging, we get very active, and we will
disable accounts and inflict other penalties as necessary.

Our policy document may be viewed at http://www.cis.yale.edu/policy.html. 
We're happy to get feedback.

-Martin

