A previous post debates the ability of the state to impose various forms of
censorship vis the charter of rights.  The government makes the laws, and
the government can and does ignore the laws as and when it chooses.  Exhibit
one might be the Pearson Airport deal, in which the government decided to 
exempt itself from legal action for breach of contract.  In many cases, the
law itself is vague or contradictory, so governments are free to apply it as
they see fit.  Any discussion of whether the government _can_ do a thing is
pointless.  Within Canada, the government can do whatever it wants. 


