From caf-talk Caf Dec 10 15:35:32 1994
From: dxb37@po.CWRU.Edu (Dean Boland)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Post-Office Sleight of Hand, The Bomb that Never? dropped?
Date: 10 Dec 1994 20:35:29 GMT
Message-ID: <3cd3eh$b78@usenet.INS.CWRU.Edu>


What is with the Post-office declining to print a stamp which
pictures the mushroom cloud of the atomic bomb over Hiroshima
and proclaims the victory that the bomb decisively won.  
I am not unsensitive to those that died in that battle or under
the radiated sky of that explosion.  But, hold on one minute here.
I am glad the bomb was dropped.  Certainly then I must be 
anti-Japanese, anti-people, war-mongerer (Throw in any you like)
But, consider the alternative to the dropping of that bomb.
Before we do, recall exactly who attacked who first in this
situation.  Bodies, Blood and Battleships strewn all over 
Pearl Harbor ring a bell?  

I like the American way of life with all of its warts.  I do
not want to be under the control of the Nazi or Japanese 
government.  Nazi government dislike is obvious.  But, the 
Japanese government is not to my liking either.  We are
Americans and we should be proud of our country in the 
sense of the freedom in which we live and the opportunity
for success that we have here.  That is not to say, love
it or leave it.  In fact, I would rather people stay and
work to change what is wrong.  

Consider your choice between dropping the bomb and living
as we do now or not dropping the bomb and what that could've
meant.

It could've meant that we AND THE JAPANESE lose up to 
1 million total soldiers in a gruesome fight to invade
the country and slog across the grassland and farmland
fighting city by city to capture the country and win the war.
Or, worse, it could've meant the loss of the war and life
under Nazi rule or the rule of a far-away Japanese 
government.

Death is not great, but the victory over the Japanese was a
monumental event in this century and should be celebrated.
-- 
Dean.

