In article <3cd3eh$b78@usenet.INS.CWRU.Edu> dxb37@po.CWRU.Edu (Dean
Boland) writes:
| 
| What is with the Post-office declining to print a stamp which
| pictures the mushroom cloud of the atomic bomb over Hiroshima
| and proclaims the victory that the bomb decisively won.

I believe that no one has a right to not be offended (and, I might
add, a right to prevent me from splitting infinitives).  On the other
hand, if by my actions I can reasonably avoid offending others
unnecessarily, I'll do so.

It seems to me that depicting the dropping of the atom bomb on
Hiroshima on a postage stamp is simply unnecessarily tasteless.  The
dropping of the atom bomb (arguably) ended the war.  It also killed
many, many thousands of civilian Japanese instantly and many more
slowly and painfully, and it ushered in several dozen years of cold
war paranoia.  I'm not sure it's something to be wholly proud of.  And
I guess I don't understand what sort of glory you or others must see
in this victory to have that overshadow the... uh... darker side of
the whole business.

I figure that a better symbol of pride in the American way of life
would be found in some more apt historical event than the annihilation
of people whose dictatorial government posed a threat at some point in
the past, no?

Why not save the "glory in warfare" myth, and the associated
propaganda, for when we're actually involved in a serious,
demoralizing war and could really use it?

Kevin <buhr@stat.wisc.edu>

